Решение Задания №1 - check-relative-links.py +
To integrate the provided Python script into your CI pipeline, you need to add a job that executes the script and fails the build if it returns a non-zero exit code. Below are configurations for the most common CI systems.
1. GitHub Actions
Add this job to your .github/workflows/ci.yml. It is designed to run on every push and pull request.
yamlКопировать
name: CI

on:
  push:
    branches: [ main ]
  pull_request:

jobs:
  check-links:
    name: Check relative Markdown links
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - name: Checkout repository
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Set up Python
        uses: actions/setup-python@v5
        with:
          python-version: '3.11'

      - name: Install dependencies (if any)
        run: pip install -r requirements.txt
        # If you have no requirements.txt, this step can be removed.

      - name: Run link checker
        run: python3 scripts/check-relative-links.py
Note: If the script fails, the run step will exit with code 1, causing the entire CI job to fail and preventing the merge.
2. GitLab CI
Add this to your .gitlab-ci.yml. It uses the official Python Docker image.
yamlКопировать
stages:
  - test

link_check:
  stage: test
  image: python:3.11-slim
  script:
    - pip install -r requirements.txt
    - python3 scripts/check-relative-links.py
  only:
    - merge_requests
    - main
3. Jenkins (Declarative Pipeline)
Add this stage to your Jenkinsfile.
groovyКопировать
pipeline {
    agent any
    stages {
        stage('Check relative links') {
            steps {
                sh 'python3 scripts/check-relative-links.py'
            }
        }
    }
    post {
        failure {
            echo 'Broken relative links detected. Check the job logs for details.'
        }
    }
}
4. Local pre-commit hook (Optional)
To catch broken links before they even reach CI, you can add a local Git hook. Create a file named .git/hooks/pre-commit:
bashКопировать
#!/bin/bash
python3 scripts/check-relative-links.py
STATUS=$?
if [ $STATUS -ne 0 ]; then
  echo "Commit rejected: broken relative links found."
  exit 1
fi
Then make it executable: chmod +x .git/hooks/pre-commit.
Recommended improvements for the script
While the current script works, here are a few enhancements to make it more robust for a production CI environment:
•	Handle anchor links (#section): The script currently strips anchors but doesn't verify if the target file actually contains the corresponding heading. You can add a check using a library like markdown-it-py to parse the target file and ensure the ID exists.
•	Handle query parameters: The script strips ?query=string, which is correct for file system checks, but ensure this doesn't hide legitimate issues if your site generator uses queries for routing.
•	Performance: For large repositories, os.walk can be slow. Consider using pathlib.Path.rglob('*.md') for cleaner code, or caching the list of all existing files in the repo at the start of the script to avoid repeated os.path.exists calls.
•	Logging: The script prints to stdout. In CI, ensure your logging level is set so that the list of broken files is clearly visible in the job summary. You could also add an option to output the results as JSON or JUnit XML for better CI integration.

